# Gorilla Firefox - Windows x86_64 build configuration
#
# Consumed by stage 40. Do not edit MOZ_BUILD_DATE by hand; the harness pins it
# from config/versions.lock.json so that two builds of the same revision produce
# the same output.

# Objdir lives OUTSIDE the source tree, at a deliberately short path.
#
# Not a preference - a hard requirement on Windows. mozmake stats target paths
# UNRESOLVED, with the '..\..\..' still in them. The deepest object in the tree
# is reached from toolkit/library/build as:
#
#   <objdir>\toolkit\library\build\..\..\..\third_party\libwebrtc\modules
#   \congestion_controller\goog_cc_scream_network_controller
#   \goog_cc_scream_network_controller_gn\Unified_cpp_etwork_controller_gn0.obj
#
# That relative part alone is 192 characters (goog_cc_scream_network_controller
# appears twice), so the objdir root must stay under about 68 characters.
#
# With the objdir inside the source tree it was
#   C:\Users\<you>\Documents\Gorilla.firefox\src\obj-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc
# is about 74 chars for a typical user directory, giving 266 total -
# six over MAX_PATH. The build failed with
# "No rule to make target ...Unified_cpp_etwork_controller_gn0.obj" even though
# the file existed, because make's stat of the unresolved path failed. The
# RESOLVED path was only 235, which is why ls found it and the error made no
# sense. Enabling long paths does not help: mozmake does not use the extended
# API for this.
mk_add_options MOZ_OBJDIR=C:/gfobj

ac_add_options --enable-application=browser
ac_add_options --target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compilation cache
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Linux gorilla mozconfig has this and it was NOT carried across when this
# file was first written. Result: configure reported "checking for ccache...
# not found" and eight consecutive builds recompiled from scratch, with
# sccache.exe sitting unused in ~/.mozbuild (mach bootstrap fetches it).
#
# TO ENABLE - uncomment the line below. Ordering matters:
#
#   1. Finish and PACKAGE a good build first. Changing this alters the
#      compiler invocation, so mach reconfigures and every existing object is
#      invalidated - you pay one full rebuild.
#   2. Uncomment, then run: gorilla build
#   3. That build is full-length while the cache populates. Every build after
#      it is dramatically faster, which is the whole point.
#
# Optional tuning (sccache defaults to a 10 GB cache; Firefox benefits from
# more headroom if the disk allows):
#   export SCCACHE_CACHE_SIZE="30G"
#
# ac_add_options --with-ccache=sccache

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optimisation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ac_add_options --enable-optimize
ac_add_options --enable-release
ac_add_options --disable-debug

# Debug symbols are the single largest contributor to link memory and link
# time. This machine has 64 GB so it could afford them, but we do not ship
# them, so we do not pay for them.
ac_add_options --disable-debug-symbols

# LTO and PGO are viable here - 64 GB is comfortably enough, which is not true
# of the VAIO. Leave them off for the first build so there is a clean baseline
# to compare against, then enable and measure the difference.
# ac_add_options --enable-lto=cross
# ac_add_options MOZ_PGO=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Carried from the Linux mozconfig - ENABLE WITH SCCACHE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These were in the Linux gorilla mozconfig and were missed when this file was
# first written (same omission as sccache: rewritten from memory rather than
# diffed - see "working scripts/diff_mozconfig.py").
#
# They are staged COMMENTED because each changes configure flags and would
# invalidate the objdir. Enable them TOGETHER WITH sccache above, in one
# rebuild, rather than paying for two.
#
# ac_add_options --disable-default-browser-agent   # Windows scheduled task that phones home
# ac_add_options --disable-webspeech               # speech-to-text / text-to-speech APIs
# ac_add_options --disable-necko-wifi              # WLAN scanning for geolocation
# ac_add_options --enable-rust-simd                # cross-platform performance
# ac_add_options --enable-jemalloc                 # already the Windows default; explicit
# ac_add_options --without-wasm-sandboxed-libraries
# mk_add_options AUTOCLOBBER=0                     # never clobber silently
#
# --without-wasm-sandboxed-libraries is a SECURITY TRADE, not a free win: it
# removes RLBox, which sandboxes graphite/ogg/hunspell into wasm. Smaller and
# faster, with less defence-in-depth against bugs in those parsers. Carried
# because the Linux build made the same choice deliberately.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT CARRIED from the Linux mozconfig
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each of these is a decision, recorded so it is never mistaken for an
# oversight. diff_mozconfig.py requires every dropped option to appear here.
#
#   --disable-accessibility
#       NOT carried, on purpose. On Linux this drops ATK. On Windows it
#       disables MSAA/UI Automation - the interface NVDA, JAWS and Narrator
#       use - so the browser becomes unreadable to a screen reader. The saving
#       is negligible: Firefox's Windows a11y engine is lazily instantiated
#       and only wakes on WM_GETOBJECT, so compiled-in-but-dormant costs a
#       little binary size and essentially no memory or CPU. Not bloat.
#
#   --enable-default-toolkit=cairo-gtk3-wayland   Wayland; meaningless here.
#   --enable-linker=lld-21                        Linux linker naming.
#   --enable-strip                                GNU strip; MSVC has no equivalent.
#   CC / CXX / AR / NM / RANLIB / OBJCOPY / OBJDUMP = "*-21"
#                                                 Linux clang-21 paths. Windows
#                                                 uses clang-cl, resolved by the
#                                                 harness (lib/toolchain.py).
#   CFLAGS / CXXFLAGS "-O3 -march=native -pipe -fno-semantic-interposition
#   -fno-plt -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections"
#                                                 GCC/Clang driver syntax; not
#                                                 accepted by clang-cl.
#   LDFLAGS "-Wl,-O2 -Wl,--as-needed -Wl,--gc-sections"
#                                                 GNU ld syntax.
#   MALLOC_CONF "narenas:8,..."                   Linux jemalloc runtime tuning.
#   RUSTFLAGS "-C target-cpu=native"              Deliberate: 'native' would tie
#                                                 the binary to THIS machine's
#                                                 CPU. The fleet includes Sandy
#                                                 and Ivy Bridge, which lack
#                                                 F16C/RDRAND - such a build
#                                                 would SIGILL there.
#   MOZ_MAKE_FLAGS "-j6"                          Parallelism is set by the
#                                                 harness from the calibrated
#                                                 thermal profile, not here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trim what we do not ship
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ac_add_options --disable-tests
ac_add_options --disable-updater
ac_add_options --disable-crashreporter
ac_add_options --disable-maintenance-service
ac_add_options --disable-parental-controls

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Branding
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Matches the Linux gorilla mozconfig exactly. The branding directory is laid
# down by stage 30 from 08.Look/NEW_FILES (81 files), and the 235 locale
# patches in that same group rename the product throughout the UI. Nothing
# here needs to be invented - it is already done.
ac_add_options --disable-official-branding
ac_add_options --with-branding=browser/branding/gorilla

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notes on what is deliberately NOT here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Linux gorilla patchset's 01.MEDIA and 02.GPU groups have no Windows
# analogue. VA-API/VDPAU maps to DXVA2/D3D11VA via Media Foundation, and
# Mesa/i915 tuning maps to ANGLE/D3D11 - both already wired up in Gecko and
# both aimed at hardware far older than this machine. The portable half of the
# patchset (networking, prefs, AI removal, theme, remote lockout, telemetry
# kill) is applied as patches in stage 30, not as configure flags.
